  "body": "<p>The last journey into Babel’s mist had ended with the company carrying Scribonia home. This time they returned with a way to protect their minds. They reached the jungle beyond the shore without the visions that had scattered them before. It was a real improvement, and it was not enough.</p><p>The ruins ahead were occupied. By the time the company understood how closely they were being surrounded, the ground itself had become part of the ambush. Their retreat would save four companions and leave <a href=\"#ryvyt\">Ryvyt</a> behind.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-different-protection\">A way through the visions</h2><p>The day at <a href=\"#skylight-refuge\">Skylight Refuge</a> was dreary. The mist remained, and Olokun brought Alyhotep up to date on a land whose dangers the newcomer had not yet learned. Ryvyt returned with an apology for his absence. Scribonia stayed because Olokun was going, even while admitting that he was struggling to find a path forward.</p><p>Mapping beyond the mountains was considered. So were flight, powerful wind and equipment that might let people avoid breathing the fog. Nymera wanted to help, but remembered what entering it had done to her. The company settled on returning to the mist with wards against its attack on the mind.</p><p>Alyhotep and his magical duplicate helped extend that protection. After the preparations were checked, four companions had their minds warded; Ryvyt did not need to breathe. Scribonia took them to the familiar shore by Teleport. They did not spend their strength fighting through the jungle on the way there.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"ropes-in-the-fog\">Ropes in the fog</h2><p>The mist had reached the edge of the beach. The Spires appeared untouched by it, as though they were warding it away. Nymera wondered whether they might be part of the answer. The observation did not yet explain how to use them or establish that the Spires and the mist shared an origin.</p><p>The company entered. This time the old fear left them alone. Visibility was another matter. Olokun and Nymera could barely see ahead, while Scribonia’s enhanced sight showed an ordinary white fog and the jungle within it. Protection from the visions had not made every companion an effective scout.</p><p>They tied themselves together. Olokun kept hold of Scribonia and reassured Nymera along the rope. She listened for danger she could not see. Ryvyt handed Olokun a flying elixir, another useful thing to carry into a situation none of them could fully predict. After roughly an hour, Scribonia spotted structures ahead.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"ruins-without-a-center\">Ruins without a safe approach</h2><p>The fog did not have the tidy center Scribonia had hoped to find. It was pressed against the mountains, with the company now deep enough to be near a source if there was a single source at all. The structures offered something concrete to investigate, not proof that they had found the cause.</p><p>Alyhotep sensed an active lair. Nymera noticed enormous creature-made marks in the ground. Ryvyt disliked the narrow spaces and hiding places. Scribonia could see the human structures clearly, but the removal of the mist’s visual interference gave him less warning of the danger the others felt.</p><p>The small buildings showed no sign of people living in them. A larger entrance lay ahead. To Scribonia it looked like a hole in a wall; to his companions its darkness carried a dreadful warning. Nymera asked whether they should return with Arjahn or Elenia. Olokun wanted to make use of how far they had come. They continued.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-guarded-entrance\">The entrance closes around them</h2><p>Scribonia recognized that the entrance mattered and that it was guarded. Ryvyt was terrified by whatever was closing around them. Scribonia answered his fear by telling him to behave like a machine. Olokun objected to the cruelty without turning the warning into a quarrel they could safely finish there.</p><p>Nymera drank the elixir Ryvyt had given her. The company readied weapons and protective magic. Then a mass of mouths, tentacles and hooves dropped in front of the entrance. Two more tongue-like horrors closed from the sides. Their approach had brought them into a prepared killing ground.</p><p>The first creature leaped. Tentacles burst from the stones beneath the company, binding them in slick green matter as the other monsters struck. From the caves came Abyss’s voice. He called the place his home and mocked their return without the whole company. He said a prisoner’s aura should have driven them back. That was his explanation; it did not identify the prisoner or settle the mist’s origin.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"four-through-the-opening\">Four through the opening</h2><p>Ryvyt was unconscious and close to death. Olokun and Nymera called for retreat. Scribonia agreed and told everyone to gather at Ryvyt so he could take them out.</p><p>The instruction depended on movement the trapped companions could not make. Nymera asked whether she was close enough. Scribonia lashed out at her hesitation, then realized that no one could move as he required. He began calculating whom he could save.</p><p>He struck the ground with his staff. The opening took Olokun, Alyhotep, Nymera and Scribonia back to the Refuge. Ryvyt was not among them. At the Refuge, the others looked around for the companion who had not come home. Olokun sat down with the recognition that another friend had been left behind, then said they needed to get him back.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"ryvyt-alone\">Ryvyt alone</h2><p>Left behind, Ryvyt believed he could rely only on himself. Alone in the green fog, he fought on, with no friends or allies beside him. Tongues and tentacles filled his sight as he fell. Ryvyt died there, separated from the people he had come to help.</p><p>At the Refuge, Scribonia tried again. Reality opened as he attempted to pull Ryvyt back. Olokun brought the hope of diamonds and immediate healing; Nymera asked Alyhotep whether he could do what had once saved her. Then the Gate failed to form.</p><p>Scribonia said Ryvyt was dead and that his own duty was fulfilled. The attempted retrieval had not brought back their companion or his remains. Their friend was dead. The failed opening had brought him no second chance. The companions’ next decisions would have to begin with the loss rather than with an escape that had saved everyone.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-life-and-the-war\">A life and the war</h2><p>Alyhotep challenged how the retreat had happened. He thought healing and Wind Walk might have given them another chance. Later he also acknowledged that the creatures might have destroyed them in gaseous form, and that his own recall could not reach companions spread so far apart. His objection preserved an alternative worth examining, not a rescue whose success had been guaranteed.</p><p>Olokun wanted Elenia and Arjahn asked for help. Scribonia made the argument larger: was bringing Ryvyt back worth the strategic cost he believed another return would carry? Olokun’s answer was yes. Nymera, Arjahn and Scribonia had all been worth saving too. Alyhotep argued that losing members weakened the company each time it was divided.</p><p>Scribonia said he had wanted everyone to understand the cost. He was tired of a war he felt they were losing. Yet he also told Olokun that his life had incalculable value. His care for one friend remained beside the harsh calculation he had applied to another. The disagreement exposed that difference without resolving it.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"what-they-carry-home\">What they carry home</h2><p>The survivors reviewed what the entrance had taught them. They had been caught together under an attack that restrained and burned them before they could establish a line. Alyhotep wanted protection prepared ahead of contact. Nymera believed she might have been able to withstand more of the assault and asked the others to trust her at the front.</p><p>Olokun called for rest, more people and a planned return to the ruins. Nymera said she already missed Ryvyt. Scribonia would not stay for the argument over what might have been done. He opened another portal and left. The passage sounded with the distant Void, but the scene did not establish his destination or a permanent change of allegiance.</p><p>Nymera turned to prayer and the preparation of a meal. The Refuge still sheltered them. The mist still covered the ruins. The wards had solved one immediate problem and failed to make the journey safe. Ryvyt’s recovery, the prisoner’s identity and a way to overcome the entrance remained unfinished work.</p><p><a href=\"#babel-mist-expedition\">Read the earlier rescue and Death’s offer</a>, or follow <a href=\"#babel-survival\">the longer struggle to keep a foothold in Babel</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"#skylight-refuge?section=skylight-recovery-council\">The next day at the Refuge: news, grief and a recovery still to attempt →</a></p>",
